WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.580
And ai writing coach trained on 100 million hours of listener reactions just launched for fiction creators. Fiction writers can now get suggestions shaped by real people who already paid to keep listening. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, episode 171. You know that feeling when you are halfway through a chapter and you cannot put your phone down? Pocket FM just released a writing helper called Sherpa that tries to create more of those moments on purpose.

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:35.040
Pocket FM is an app where you listen to stories in episodes, like a TV show you hear instead

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:40.940
of watch. Inside the app, people spend coins to unlock the next episode, the company

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:46.920
shared. Sherpa takes that same listening data and turns it into writing advice. Think of

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:52.060
it like a music app that notices the exact second you skip a song, then tells the artist

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:57.540
how to write a better hook. You bring an idea and the tool helps shape characters, plan

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:03.760
a whole season and write dialogue. It also suggests spots where a listener is most likely to keep

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:09.980
going. Writers still make the final choices. They just get instant signals about what already worked

00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:15.720
for millions of other listeners. Picture a GPS for your plot. You still pick the destination,

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:21.440
but the map warns you about the tours where most travelers quit. If you write fan fiction,

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:24.860
Tell stories on TikTok or dream up your own web series.

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:29.000
That gap between an idea and a finished season just got smaller.

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:35.120
Creative tools can now learn from audience behavior instead of handing out generic writing tips.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:42.820
Pocket FM grew from $21 million to $500 million in annual recurring revenue in just three years.

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:47.440
annual recurring revenue is the money a company can count on every year from

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:52.220
people who keep paying, like a subscription that does not stop. They got there

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:57.480
by studying exactly where listeners drop off or choose to spend coins. People in

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:01.540
the United States already spend more than two hours a day inside the app,

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:06.800
compared with less than one hour on TikTok. Shirt by uses that listening data to

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:11.020
suggest where a story should leave people hanging and how dialogue can feel

00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:15.720
more natural. The company says the goal is to help more people finish the stories they

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:20.820
have carried in their heads for years. Even with all that help, the creators still stress

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:25.700
that writers need their own taste, and a reason for anyone to care about the final result.

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:32.080
You can try the idea right now, for free, inside the Pocket FM app on your phone. Start

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:37.200
a new story project and give Sherpa a simple premise, like a teen finds a phone that

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:42.880
predicts the future. See what characters and cliffhangers it suggests, then change them until

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:48.160
they sound like you. A cliffhanger is a moment that leaves you desperate to know what happens next,

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:54.440
like a game level that ends right before the boss fight. The tool is free to experiment with inside

00:02:54.440 --> 00:03:00.340
the existing app. TikTok seems to know which video will keep you scrolling, even when you meant to

00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:06.940
stop after one. It watches tiny signals like how long you watch, whether you rewatch or if you tap

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:13.520
away fast. Sherpa copies that idea, but for long stories instead of short clips. It looks at

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:19.260
millions of moments where listeners stopped, spent coins to continue, or binged an entire season.

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:25.300
Those moments become training signals, which just means examples the AI studies so it can spot

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:31.560
patterns. The patterns cover which kinds of dialogue, twists, or character choices tend to hold attention.

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:34.660
This is not magic, and it is not mind-reading.

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:38.220
It is counting and comparing patterns at a huge scale.

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:43.340
A friend who has watched every episode of a show can often guess what will happen next

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:46.420
because they have seen the same shapes over and over.

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:51.040
The more listening hours the system sees, the sharper those guesses become.

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:56.320
Another way to see it is a basketball coach reviewing game tape from millions of matches.

00:03:56.960 --> 00:03:58.680
The coach is not inventing the sport.

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:03.180
The coaches marking the exact plays where crowds lean in or get up and leave.

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:09.340
The data includes precise timestamps showing where audiences decided to stop or pay to keep going.

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:14.060
Those exact points become the training examples that shape every new suggestion.

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:18.160
Because the data comes from real listeners who already spend coins,

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:22.160
the patterns reflect actual choices rather than made up preferences.

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:26.540
That is why the tool can point out places where a story might lose people

00:04:26.540 --> 00:04:29.260
before the writer even shares it with real readers.

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:32.780
That kind of feedback used to require months of testing

00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:33.920
with a live audience.

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:37.120
Once you see it as pattern counting at giant scale,

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:38.760
the mystery drops away,

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:41.840
and the writing advice starts to look almost ordinary.

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:45.900
A 13-year-old just built an AI that turns messy comments

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:47.480
into clear product advice.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:50.060
The project is called Feedback AI,

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:51.660
and it runs in a browser.

00:04:52.220 --> 00:04:53.940
It takes raw user comments like,

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:59.040
Like, the app is slow on my phone, then sorts them into categories, priority levels, and

00:04:59.040 --> 00:04:59.780
short summaries.

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:05.180
When a comment arrives, the AI parses it, which just means it reads the sentence and

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:06.460
pulls out the useful bits.

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:12.840
It might label a note as category performance, priority high, and summary, improved mobile

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:13.320
speed.

00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:18.680
Then it clusters similar comments together, like dropping every complaint about the same

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:20.420
broken zipper into one pile.

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:25.640
Think of a school mailroom that stamps each letter with a department, an urgency sticker,

00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:27.000
and a one-line recap.

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:31.640
The whole thing was built by someone still in middle school, which is a pretty strong

00:05:31.640 --> 00:05:35.920
reminder that you do not need to be a professional coder to make a useful AI tool.

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:41.920
The app is a non-commercial validation project, meaning it is a test, not a company selling

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:42.420
a product.

00:05:42.980 --> 00:05:48.340
It stays password protected so the creator does not run up large bills on an open AI account.

00:05:48.340 --> 00:05:54.780
Open AI is the company behind tools like chat GPT and each request to that kind of model

00:05:54.780 --> 00:05:56.000
can cost a little money.

00:05:56.540 --> 00:06:03.460
Open Feedback AI in your browser, enter the bypass code beta2026 and paste a few sample

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:05.780
comments about a game or app you use.

00:06:06.220 --> 00:06:10.520
Watch how the AI organizes them and see if the groupings make sense to you.

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:16.120
You can also test the input parser with your own made up comments and notice where it succeeds

00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:16.980
or struggles.

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:22.380
Tread it like a challenge. Write three messy complaints, then see whether the summaries

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:28.120
still sound fair. A free step-by-step guide now shows exactly how to learn large language

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:33.940
models from zero. A large language model is the kind of AI that reads and writes text.

00:06:34.340 --> 00:06:39.600
The same family of systems that power chatbots you may have already tried. The guide walks

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:45.260
through what those models actually are, how they are trained, and simple ways to start experimenting

00:06:45.260 --> 00:06:47.000
without any coding background.

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:49.100
Analytics Insight Reports

00:06:49.100 --> 00:06:51.360
It is written for complete beginners

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:53.300
and it includes clear next steps

00:06:53.300 --> 00:06:55.700
you can follow on your own laptop or phone.

00:06:56.300 --> 00:06:57.880
The article explains the difference

00:06:57.880 --> 00:06:59.520
between simply using a model

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:01.540
and understanding how it was built.

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:04.500
Using a model is like eating at a restaurant.

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:06.540
Understanding how it was built

00:07:06.540 --> 00:07:07.980
is like reading the recipe

00:07:07.980 --> 00:07:09.320
and trying the dish at home.

00:07:09.860 --> 00:07:11.980
Then it moves into practical exercises

00:07:11.980 --> 00:07:13.620
that require only a browser.

00:07:13.620 --> 00:07:18.740
sensor. Each section builds on the last, so you can move from basic prompts to more advanced

00:07:18.740 --> 00:07:24.080
experiments at your own pace. Open that free guide and pick the first hands-on section

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:29.700
that matches your level. Try one small exercise today, like chatting with a model while noticing

00:07:29.700 --> 00:07:34.960
what it gets right or wrong. The early steps are designed so someone with no prior technical

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:41.980
experience can finish them in a single afternoon. HTC's new Vive Eagle Glasses include an optional

00:07:41.980 --> 00:07:45.620
setting that pauses recording the moment anyone nearby says stop.

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:50.960
The feature is off by default, which gives people a simple way to protect privacy during

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:51.840
everyday use.

00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:57.980
Turn it on in the device menu, and any spoken request to stop immediately halts video capture

00:07:57.980 --> 00:07:59.300
with no extra buttons.

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:04.180
It is a little like a camera that covers its own lens the instant someone asks.

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:09.460
Even if AI stopped improving today, most people still have years of catching up.

00:08:09.460 --> 00:08:14.040
Current models can already do far more than most of us are using them for.

00:08:14.420 --> 00:08:16.700
The gap is not about new breakthroughs.

00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:20.140
The gap is learning how to fold these tools into school projects,

00:08:20.420 --> 00:08:22.220
creative hobbies, and daily tasks.

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:28.160
One post points out four specific advantages that help people close this gap over time,

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:33.300
starting with simply noticing where a model can already save hours on repetitive work.

